oneqnode on Debian
==================

The package installs the oneqnode node in /opt/oneq/node, either as a systemd template service (oneqnode@NAME) or as a
supervisord program (oneqnode-NAME), whichever you choose. It runs as the dedicated user "oneqnode" with only
CAP_NET_ADMIN (for the TAP interface); both paths start the same script, /opt/oneq/node/bin/run NAME. The Python libraries
are bundled. The commands oneqnode and oneqnode-init are in PATH (links to /opt/oneq/node/bin).

Requirements: Debian 13 (Python 3.13, OpenSSL >= 3.5), iproute2, /dev/net/tun.

Configuration: /etc/oneq/node.conf
----------------------------------
An INI file (comments with # or ;) read by the application: the [defaults] section applies to all nodes, and every node has
its own section, [NAME]. Command-line options override it; a misspelled option stops the node with a clear message. After a
change:

  sudo systemctl restart oneqnode@NAME     or     sudo supervisorctl restart oneqnode-NAME

Options: relays, community, user, label, key_file, password_file, ifname, listen, advertise, stun, cafile, via,
force_relay, no_relay, relay_after, configure_interface, init. Explanations and an example are in the file itself.
Keys and passwords are NOT stored in this file: only the paths of their files (owner oneqnode, mode 0600).

Setup and start
---------------
The account (community, user, password) is created by the oneqrelay administrator. Then, on the node:

  sudo oneqnode-init lan --relay https://r1.example.org --relay https://r2.example.org \
       --community lan --user alice --advertise 203.0.113.5:51820

The command generates the node key (/etc/oneq/node-lan.key), asks for the password (or --password-file FILE; it is kept in
/etc/oneq/node-lan.password), writes the [lan] section in node.conf and starts the node. The service manager is chosen
automatically: "init" from [defaults], otherwise systemd if it is PID 1, otherwise supervisord if installed; you can force
it with --init systemd|supervisor|none. The interface is called oneq-lan; the IP and MAC addresses come automatically from
oneqrelay:

  ip addr show oneq-lan
  journalctl -u oneqnode@lan -f                 (systemd)
  supervisorctl status oneqnode-lan             (supervisord; log in /var/log/oneq/node-lan.log)

--advertise: the address at which other nodes can reach this node (needed behind NAT or a firewall; without it the node
uses the relay). Several --relay options mean automatic failover.

Bound to an existing interface: put "via = IFACE" in the node's section (the interface stays owned by the process that
created it; oneqnode makes its own TAP).

Stop/remove: sudo oneqnode-init lan --remove (stops the node and deletes the section, the key and the password).
`apt purge oneqnode` deletes /etc/oneq/node.conf and the keys; `apt remove` stops the nodes but keeps the configuration.

Educational project, without independent cryptographic audit.
